@page "/events/example/plain-components"

@rendermode InteractiveWebAssembly

<ExampleDetail Title="Plain Components"
               SubTitle="Events"
               ComponentName="Events"
               PageSourceUrl="@RForgeHelpers.RepoDocsUrl("/RForgeDocs.Client/Pages/Examples/Events/PlainComponentsPage.razor")"
               ComponentDetailsUrl="/events/details" />

<div class="columns">
    <div class="column is-5">
        <article class="section">
            <div class="box">
                <div class="content">
                    <ExampleHeader Level="Simple" Mode="Interactive WebAssembly">
                        Components that can't derive from <code>RfEventComponent</code> (third-party or legacy components) can still
                        read hub state through an ordinary <code>[CascadingParameter]</code>.
                    </ExampleHeader>

                    <HowItWorks>
                        <p><strong>Try it:</strong> change each theme. The two cards don't affect each other.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>PlainThemedCard</code> is a plain <code>ComponentBase</code> with <code>[CascadingParameter] ThemeState? Theme</code>. It knows nothing about the hub.</li>
                            <li>In the first card, <code>options.CascadeRootState&lt;ThemeState&gt;()</code> (see <code>EventsDemoHub.Configure</code>) exposes the <em>root</em> theme as a <code>CascadingValueSource&lt;ThemeState&gt;</code>.</li>
                            <li>In the second card, <code>&lt;StateScope TState="ThemeState" CascadeValue="true"&gt;</code> provides a <em>separate</em> instance to its subtree and also cascades it.</li>
                            <li><code>ThemePicker</code> resolves the nearest provider, so the same component writes the root theme in the first card and the scoped theme in the second.</li>
                            <li>Trade-off: cascade consumers re-render on <em>every</em> change. Event-aware consumers can use selectors (<code>ThemePicker</code> only watches <code>Name</code>).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </HowItWorks>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
    </div>
    <div class="column">
        <article class="section">
            <section class="card mb-5">
                <header class="card-header"><p class="card-header-title">Root theme&nbsp;<code>CascadeRootState&lt;ThemeState&gt;()</code></p></header>
                <div class="card-content">
                    <ThemePicker />
                    <PlainThemedCard Caption="Plain component reading the root theme" />
                </div>
            </section>
            <section class="card">
                <header class="card-header"><p class="card-header-title">Scoped theme&nbsp;<code>&lt;StateScope CascadeValue="true"&gt;</code></p></header>
                <div class="card-content">
                    <StateScope TState="ThemeState" Initial="ThemeState.Ocean" CascadeValue="true">
                        <ThemePicker />
                        <PlainThemedCard Caption="Plain component inside the StateScope" />
                    </StateScope>
                </div>
            </section>
        </article>
    </div>
</div>
